    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2826.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 749,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to understand water truly, you have to look at it through a few different lenses: its unique physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (Why Water is Weird and Wonderful)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (rivers, oceans), **solid** (ice, snow), and **gas** (steam, water vapor). \n\nIt also has several unusual properties that make it unique among liquids:\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it carries valuable chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes.\n* **Ice Floats:** When most liquids freeze, their molecules pack tightly together, making the solid denser than the liquid. Water is the opposite. When water freezes into ice, its molecules form an open hexagonal structure that takes up *more* space, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice cubes float, and it is why lakes freeze from the top down, allowing aquatic life to survive the winter underneath.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which is how water travels up the roots of tall trees).\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet, often called the \"Blue Planet.\"\n* **Abundance:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by the oceans.\n* **Distribution:** Roughly 96.5% of all Earth's water is salt water in the oceans. Only 2.5% is fresh water, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through a system called the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from the oceans and land, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into the oceans via rivers and streams. The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n* **Biological Medium:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. It acts as a shock absorber for the brain and spinal cord, regulates body temperature (through sweat), and flushes out waste.\n* **Chemical Reactions:** Water is the medium in which almost all the metabolic and cellular processes of life take place.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species, from microscopic plankton to the blue whale, the largest animal on Earth.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, shaping our landscapes, regulating our climate, and making the existence of living things possible."
